<body>
    <h1>P-values and other significance pitfalls</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable">
  <colgroup>
   <col>
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <h1 title=3D"" id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-Summary"><str=
ong>Summary<br></strong></h1>
     <p title=3D"">P-values used in null hypothesis significance testing (N=
HST) can be extremely misleading. Not only have these p-values by themselve=
s some strong theoretical and practical limitations (discussed in 1.2 below=
), but, to make things worse, the interpretation of p-values itself is very=
 often wrong, as will be shown in 1.1.</p>
     <p title=3D"">Allied to their already extensive limitations, such comm=
on incorrect interpretations about the meaning of the p-value often form a =
toxic cocktail that results in spurious results being considered significan=
t. The abuse of p-values has largely contributed to the reproducibility cri=
sis in science. That reproducibility crisis is particularly acute in domain=
s with small sample sizes and rather relaxed and liberal use of the p-value=
, such as social sciences, but it also affects other domains where too much=
 meaning tends to be invested in limited one-off experiments.</p>
     <p title=3D"">If p-values have to be used, to limit their damage, it i=
s recommended that no 'sig<span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;">nificant' =
meaning be ever attributed to any p-value &gt; 0.005. Instead any p-value i=
n the 0.005-0.05 range should be deemed just 'suggestive', and even a 'sign=
ificant' p-value (=E2=89=A4 0.005) should still be carefully handled (1.4.d=
).&nbsp;</span></p>
     <p title=3D"">Various alternatives to the p-value have been proposed. =
By large and far, most suggestions draw on a Bayesian approach, while still=
 being able to offer conservative estimates with very reasonable minimal as=
sumptions about priors. The False Positive Risk with its minimal estimate (=
1.4.c), or the related Bayes Factor, are the most common robust alternative=
s put forward.</p>
     <p title=3D"">More generally, any significance test needs to be evalua=
ted in the context of possible p-hacking, of possible structural bias in th=
e models or in the data being used. Without such context checks, a signific=
ance test may just be the tip of a very weak experimental chain. Its contri=
bution to establishing 'significance' will then just be as weak as the weak=
est part of the chain (see 'mirage of deductive reasoning' in 1.1 and also =
sections 2, 3 &amp; 4).</p>
     <p title=3D"">Once all has been duly considered, it then becomes appar=
ent that p-values and the blind application of any statistical test may suf=
fer from a 'mirage of objectivity' (1.2.b). In that sense, not only is the =
Bayesian requirement for some minimal statement about priors a methodologic=
al requirement, but it also represents a sound antidote to such misleading =
claims to objectivity.&nbsp;</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1P-Values"><strong style=3D"l=
etter-spacing: -0.01em;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">1&nbsp; </span>&nbs=
p;P-Values</strong></h1>
<h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1.1Commonmisinterpretations:"=
><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;color:#c25100;">1.1</span><span style=3D"=
color:#09326c;"><strong> Common misinterpretations:</strong></span></h2>
<p>The following claims about p-values are extremely common and also absolu=
tely wrong:</p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">'The p-value is the probability that th=
e result observed occurred by chance'</span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">'The p-value is the probability that th=
e null hypothesis is true'</span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">'The result is significant because the =
p-value is below 0.05'</span></li>
</ul>
<p><br>
  A related methodological claim, made when using p-values, is also wrong a=
nd shows a misunderstanding of the scientific method (which could be called=
 the '<strong>mirage of deductive reasoning</strong>'):</p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">The&nbsp; probability of a conclusion b=
eing in error can be calculated from the data in a single isolated experime=
nt, without reference to the full experimental path trodden, and without re=
ference to external evidence or plausibility of any underlying mechanism.</=
span></li>
</ul>
<p><br>
  In fact, p-values by themselves are of limited value. We need another sta=
tistical step, which may require some degree of judgment, <em>plus some met=
hodological care</em> to make sense of a p-value.</p>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"content-wrapper" title=3D"">
 <h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1.2Limitations:"><strong><sp=
an style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">1.2</span>&nbsp;=
</span></strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>Limitations:</strong=
></span></h2>
 <h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-a.Thep-valuedoesnotanswerthe=
rightquestion:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color=
:#c25100;">a.</span> The p-value does not answer the right question:</stron=
g></span></h3>
</div>
<p title=3D"">The p value is a statement about p(data/H0) when what we are =
actually after is a statement about p(H0/data).</p>
<p title=3D"">In Bayesian notations, the two quantities are linked by the r=
atio p(H0)/p(data):<br>
  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; eq1:<em>&nbsp; &n=
bsp;p</em><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">(H0/data) =3D p(H0)/p(data) * p(da=
ta/H0).&nbsp;</span></p>
<p title=3D""><br>
  The difference between the two can be described as:</p>
<ul title=3D"">
 <li>the difference between inductive reasoning (p(H0/data)) and deductive =
reasoning (p(data/H0)),</li>
 <li>the error of the transposed conditional, a.k.a. Prosecutor's Fallacy.<=
/li>
</ul>
<p>Another formulation of the issue in purely deductive terms may be:</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;"=
>Knowing that the data are =E2=80=98rare=E2=80=99 when there is no true dif=
ference (p(data/H0)) is of little use unless one determines whether or not =
they are also =E2=80=98rare=E2=80=99 when there is a true difference (p(dat=
a/H1)).</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D""><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable">
  <colgroup>
   <col>
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e3fcef confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light green 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e3fcef">
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">He is a simple illustrati=
on of the problem:<br></span><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><br>
        Let's consider a population made in equal parts of 30 year-old male=
s and 13 year-old ones. <br>
        Let H0 be the hypothesis that based on its weight a given individua=
l in that population is a 13 year-old teenager, H1 the reverse hypothesis t=
hat it is a 30 year-old male.<br></span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Let's say that 95% of 13-=
year old males weighs less than 75kg, and 3% between 70 and 75kg. (Our dist=
ribution is given in step of 5 kg)</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">If a random individual we=
ighs between 70 and 75kg, what does the p-value tell us?<br></span><span st=
yle=3D"color:#172b4d;">The conclusion based on the p-value threshold of 0.0=
5 supposing H0 would be that H1 is validated. i.e. that the individual is (=
likely) a 30 year-old adult, and not a 13 year-old teenager.</span><br><br>=
<span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">But how likely? In other words what is P(H0/=
data)?</span><br><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Let's suppose that 25% of 3=
0-year old males weighs between 70 and 75kg. </span><br><span style=3D"colo=
r:#172b4d;">So 14% of the whole population is in the 70-75kg bucket, with 1=
2.5% (25%/2) contribution from the 30 year-old subset and 1.5% (3%/2) from =
the 13 year-old subset.</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Hence an individual in th=
e 70-75kg bucket has a 12.5%/14% =3D 89% chance of being a 30 year-old.<br>
        We still effectively have <strong>11%</strong> of wrongly calling t=
hat individual an adult (i.e.: concluding that H1 is true), when it is not =
(H0 is actually true). That's our false positive risk and it is not 5%.</sp=
an></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">In particular note that t=
here is no way to answer the proper question p(H0/data) without considering=
 the 30-year old weight distribution, while the p-value itself is based onl=
y on the 13-year old weight distribution.</span></p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p title=3D"">This deductive representation of the issue is <a href=3D"http=
s://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.171085" class=3D"external-l=
ink" rel=3D"nofollow">illustrated below</a> (note: y0 =3D 0.0526, not 0.526=
 as indicated in figure text).</p>
<p title=3D"">There one can see that p(data/H0) does not mean that H1 is mu=
ch less likely than H0 given a high value for p(data/H0). <br>
  With a low power test, under H1 the probability that the test value t (us=
ing a Student test for small populations) is under the critical level (2.04=
) is still relatively high at 22%, against 95% for H0.&nbsp;</p>
<p title=3D""><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-em=
bedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"f=
alse" width=3D"468" src=3D"d881211a6efdcacaa099d8d9126ac283062bb25fc0659dc1=
16bfbb32970bcb3e" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image20=
22-5-10_14-4-34.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1652148276294&amp;ap=
i=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"2550=
54965" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"atta=
chment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-10_14-4-34.png" d=
ata-base-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource=
-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567"=
 data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" hei=
ght=3D"350"></span></p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-b.Thechanceofafalsereportedef=
fectisoftenveryhigh:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D=
"color:#c25100;">b.</span> The chance of a false reported effect is often v=
ery high</strong></span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>:</strong></=
span></h3>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">In section 1.4 we shall introduce the Fal=
se Positive Risk (FPR), also called False Discovery Risk, which is the prob=
ability of wrongly concluding that there is a size effect (P(H1) is true) w=
hen there is none (P(H0) is actually true).&nbsp;<br></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">A popular Nature article made that point =
through a simple illustration reproduced here:<br></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;"><span clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img=
 class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" width=3D"468" src=
=3D"2512f102991e244b646ff107ae5badbabd9e804005ffabc94f9aac5b0775cb0b" data-=
image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image-2025-1-29_11-43-46.png?v=
ersion=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1738104226802&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolv=
ed-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1548782705" data-linked-r=
esource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-=
resource-default-alias=3D"image-2025-1-29_11-43-46.png" data-base-url=3D"ht=
tps://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"i=
mage/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-linked-resou=
rce-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" height=3D"266"></span=
></span></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">As we can see, at the very neutral a-prio=
ri P(H1) =3D P(H0) =3D 0.5 level (a realistic best case for any new discove=
ry setup), the False Positive Risk is <u>at least:</u></span></p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">a whopping ~29% for a p-value of 0.05 a=
nd </span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">still a large ~11% for a p-value of 0.0=
1.&nbsp;</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">We will explain in 1.4 below how these va=
lues are derived via a simple equation. Here are more illustrative values f=
or the FPR in the meantime:</span></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable">
  <colgroup>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
   <col>
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th colspan=3D"14" class=3D"confluenceTh">Supposing conservative P(H1) =
=3D P(H0) =3D 0.5</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e6fcff confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light teal 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e6fcff"><strong title=3D"">p-va=
lue</strong></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">1E-04</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.001</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.005</td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fff0b3 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 100%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fff0b3"><strong>0.01</strong=
></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.02</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.03</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.04</td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fff0b3 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 100%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fff0b3"><strong>0.05</strong=
></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.06</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.07</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.08</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.09</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.1</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e6fcff confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light teal 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e6fcff"><strong title=3D"">mini=
mal FPR</strong></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">0.2%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">1.8%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">6.7%</td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fff0b3 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 100%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fff0b3"><strong>11.1%</stron=
g></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">17.5%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">22.2%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">25.9%</td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fff0b3 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 100%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fff0b3"><strong>28.9%</stron=
g></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">31.5%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">33.6%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">35.5%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">37.1%</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">38.5%</td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-c.Thep-valuecomfortsanillusio=
nofobjectivity:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"colo=
r:#c25100;">c.</span> The p-value comforts a</strong></span><span style=3D"=
color:#09326c;"><strong>n illusion of objectivity:</strong></span></h3>
<p title=3D"">Unfortunately there is simply no way to obtain a p(H0/data) f=
rom p(data/H0) without some a priori knowledge about p(H0) (equivalently p(=
H1)). Such a-priori knowledge may seem to inject a level of subjectivity wh=
ich is typically reviled in some statistical circles (especially in the pre=
 big-data area).<br><br>
  This simple potential issue has largely contributed to the misuse of the =
p-value against better judgement (and the warnings of Fisher himself), but =
the reality is that explicit subjectivity is far preferable to the status q=
uo of a pretend objectivity pervading common implicit misinterpretations of=
 p(data/H0). This situation may be called the '<strong>mirage of objectivit=
y</strong>'.</p>
<p title=3D"">This has over time become a major hindrance to reproducibilit=
y in some scientific domains where the p-value is overused.&nbsp;<br>
  In any case in many situations some useful bound can be obtained for p(H0=
/data) without having to make any unreasonable assumption for p(H0), which =
effectively can lift the reluctance of having to deal with subjectivity.</p=
>
<p title=3D""><br></p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-d.Anisolatedstatisticaltestof=
anykindcaneasilymissthebiggerpicture:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><stro=
ng><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">d.</span> An isolated statistical test of=
 any kind can easily miss the bigger picture:</strong></span></h3>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">The probability that a Hypothesis Test-ba=
sed research finding is true or not depends on<br></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">1. Statist=
ical power<br>
   2. Level of statistical significance<br>
   3. PRIOR probability of it being true (before you even thought of your s=
tudy)</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">and more generally<br></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span styl=
e=3D"color:#172b4d;">4. Overall experiment setup, including non-productive =
avenues followed</span><br><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">5. Further valida=
tion via alternate experiments</span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><br></p>
<p title=3D""><br></p>
<div class=3D"content-wrapper" title=3D"">
 <h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-e.Thep-valueisactuallyverybr=
ittle:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100=
;">e.</span> The p-value is actually very brittle:<br></strong></span></h3>
</div>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>P-value prediction interval for e=
xisting study:&nbsp;</strong></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">The limitation of a </span><em style=3D"t=
ext-align: justify;"><span>p</span></em>-<span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">val=
ue can be better understood by adding information about the uncertainty of =
this parameter.</span><em style=3D"text-align: justify;"><span><br></span><=
/em>P-<span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">value <span class=3D"inline-comment-ma=
rker" data-ref=3D"0cb32882-7a0d-476e-8e87-724b34f4eb06"><strong><a style=3D=
"color: rgb(51,102,255);" href=3D"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10=
.1098/rsbl.2019.0174" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">prediction i=
ntervals</a></strong></span> for the <u>undertaken study</u> or a <u>future=
 replication study</u> are a way to achieve this. </span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">This can be accomplished using an online =
calculator for both types of studies.<br></span><span style=3D"color:#172b4=
d;"><br>
   For example, in the first scenario (undertaken study)</span></p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">if</span><em style=3D"text-align: justi=
fy;"><span> </span></em>p-value <span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">=3D 0.01, it=
 will have a 95% prediction interval of [0.000006, 0.54] for the undertaken=
 study</span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">if</span><em style=3D"text-align: justi=
fy;"><span> </span></em>p-value <span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">=3D 0.0001, =
the 95% prediction interval is [0, 0.05] for the undertaken study</span></l=
i>
</ul>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">We can see that the width of these predic=
tion intervals is typically surprisingly large as compared to the naked sin=
gle<em style=3D"text-align: justify;"> </em>p<em style=3D"text-align: justi=
fy;">-</em>value.</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>P-value prediction interval for r=
eplication study:&nbsp;</strong></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">For p-value =3D 0.0001, the 95% predictio=
n interval of a future replication study is [0, 0.26] (to be compared to [0=
, 0.05] for the undertaken study).</span></p>
<p>Reproducibility is the heart of science, but as we can see from the abov=
e example, we have to agree with <a href=3D"https://www.allpsych.uni-giesse=
n.de/journalclub/pdf/Cumming.PerspPsychSci.2008.pdf" class=3D"external-link=
" rel=3D"nofollow">Cumming's conclusion</a>:</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">If my expe=
riment results in p-value =3D .05, for example, what p is an exact replicat=
ion =E2=80=94 with a new sample of participants [of the same size] =E2=80=
=94 likely to give?</span><br><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">Surprisingly, =
the answer is =E2=80=98=E2=80=98<strong>Pretty much anything</strong>.=E2=
=80=99</span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">=E2=80=99</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><br>
   Note that the prediction interval for a replication study of the same si=
ze as the existing one is independent of that study actual sample size.&nbs=
p; All that matters is the ratio of sample size of replicating study to ori=
ginal study.<br>
   In the same way, the p-value prediction interval for an undertaken study=
 is not dependent on the size of the sample.</span></p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" width=
=3D"450" src=3D"300434bcd68a0be517c9fb99e8fedf0e8c5e088b94684148daed31fe7c0=
fb67f" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image2022-5-20_16-=
16-8.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1653020169506&amp;api=3Dv2" dat=
a-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"257998905" data=
-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" dat=
a-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-20_16-16-8.png" data-base-ur=
l=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-ty=
pe=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-linke=
d-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" height=3D"449"=
></span><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded=
-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" =
width=3D"468" src=3D"9ea7753ffa3b45136d4f1fd7e2b8ba3d129626c7b1f5f7e07d360e=
d730bc4f6e" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image2022-5-2=
0_16-25-37.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1653020738981&amp;api=3Dv=
2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"257998950=
" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachmen=
t" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-20_16-25-37.png" data-=
base-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-con=
tent-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" dat=
a-linked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" height=
=3D"392"></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><strong>Simulations:</strong></p>
<p>This brittleness is also well illustrated by <a href=3D"https://royalsoc=
ietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsbl.2019.0174" class=3D"external-link" rel=
=3D"nofollow">some simulations</a> (see also <a href=3D"https://www.allpsyc=
h.uni-giessen.de/journalclub/pdf/Cumming.PerspPsychSci.2008.pdf" class=3D"e=
xternal-link" rel=3D"nofollow">this paper</a>):<br><span class=3D"confluenc=
e-embedded-file-wrapper"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=
=3D"false" src=3D"6e53a4eaf921d150d0f27ac45f18cc53f6f990cc416dc7d5c278dae19=
5b960ac" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image2022-5-17_1=
5-3-27.png?version=3D2&amp;modificationDate=3D1669774228923&amp;api=3Dv2" d=
ata-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"257985989" da=
ta-linked-resource-version=3D"2" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" d=
ata-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-17_15-3-27.png" data-base-=
url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-=
type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-lin=
ked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"468=
" height=3D"404"></span></p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-f.Thereisnoone-fit-allsignifi=
cancelevel:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c=
25100;">f.</span> There is no one-fit-all significance level:</strong></spa=
n></h3>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">Note that </span><a href=3D"https://www.n=
ature.com/articles/s41562-017-0189-z" style=3D"color: rgb(34,34,34);" class=
=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">different research domains</a><span sty=
le=3D"color:#37471f;"> have long use different significance thresholds.</sp=
an></p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">In parti=
cle-physics at the CERN, new discoveries typically follow a 5 sigma rule (p=
-value ~ 0.0000003) and often use 2 sets of independent detectors managed b=
y different teams.</span></span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">In genom=
ics, a genome-wide significance threshold=E2=80=99 of 0.00000005 is commonl=
y used.</span></span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">drug testing typically requires multipl=
e independent tests, and often the final validation (or invalidation!) of t=
he size-effect comes over the years from meta analysis across multiple stud=
ies.</span></li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a typical example of medical meta-study:</p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper"><img class=3D"confluenc=
e-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" src=3D"0fcd9ce931c86a437514234563c3e0=
a5b7911fa33d02bf9fce9afcff6e777d05" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments=
/255050567/image2022-5-17_16-28-44.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1=
652761726249&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-=
resource-id=3D"257986664" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-re=
source-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-=
5-17_16-28-44.png" data-base-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" =
data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-contai=
ner-id=3D"255050567" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabinde=
x=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"468" height=3D"441"></span>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">Such meta-studies were incidentally one o=
f the possible correct use of p-values considered by Fisher, who insisted o=
n the fact that its own a single positive significance-test at 0.05 level c=
ould only be <u>suggestive</u>, and should only <u>encourage</u> the experi=
menters to further check the possible size effect.</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">=E2=80=9CP=
ersonally, the writer prefers to set a low standard of significance at the =
5 percent point.<br>
   A scientific fact should be regarded as experimentally established only =
if a properly designed experiment rarely fails to give this level of signif=
icance.=E2=80=9D<br>
   Ronald Fisher, Statistical Methods for Research Workers, 1925.</span></p=
>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable">
  <colgroup>
   <col>
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">An interesting implication of this c=
onsideration of the use of the p-value in different domains is that <strong=
>the p-value has been already commonly been informed by a degree of subject=
ivity</strong>, based on the cost of errors in the confusion matrix (see me=
dical drugs approval in particular) and the typical prior odds in each doma=
in.</span></p>
     <p><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">There is thus no point pretending th=
at p-values are an objective tool that does not require any prior by contra=
st to Bayesian methods, since successful applications have grounded themsel=
ves in their domain priors.</span><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;"> </span></=
p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><strong><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">&nbsp;</span></strong></p>
<h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1.3P-value,testpowerandsample=
size:"><strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;=
">1.3</span> P-value, t</span></strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><stro=
ng>est power and sample size:</strong></span></h2>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-a.Theroleofthetestpower"><spa=
n style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100;"><br>
     a. </span></strong></span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>The r=
ole of the test power&nbsp;</strong></span></h3>
<p title=3D"">Note that the key element in the Null Hypothesis Significance=
 Testing setup is the <strong>power</strong> of the statistical test.<br>
  That power is typically linked to the size of the populations for which s=
ome statistics are being compared.</p>
<p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>Small populations (low=
 double digit ones typically) are very prone to p-value abuses as they deli=
ver low-power tests.</strong></span><br><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;"><spa=
n style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>For higher populations the p-value taken=
 of its own is much less likely to mislead, but this is not a reason to sti=
ll confuse p(data/H0) with p(H0/data).</strong></span><br></span></p>
<p title=3D"">Basically we do not want to be right part of the time (large =
populations) for the wrong reason (convergence of p(data/H0) and p(H0/data)=
 in that case).&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-b.[pi]intervalandpowerinterva=
l:"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">b=
. </span></strong></span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>[pi] interv=
al and </strong><strong>power interval:</strong></span><span style=3D"color=
:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><br></strong></span></spa=
n></h3>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">The p-value prediction interval was intro=
duced in 1.2 as a way to better appreciate how brittle a p-value may be. <b=
r><a href=3D"https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=3DAONE&amp;u=3Dgooglescholar&amp=
;id=3DGALE|A441882875&amp;v=3D2.1&amp;it=3Dr&amp;sid=3DgoogleScholar&amp;as=
id=3D2d74e422" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Lazzeroni <em>et al=
</em></a> adds other intervals to the p-value prediction interval:</span></=
p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#0c66e4;"><strong>[pi] interval:</strong></span> =
P-value confidence interval for the "true population P value" or [pi] value=
, which is defined as the value of P when parameter estimates equal their u=
nknown population values, noted&nbsp;</li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#0c66e4;"><strong>power interval:</strong></span>=
 confidence interval for the de facto power of a study, which is the probab=
ility of rejecting the null hypothesis given the true, but unknown, populat=
ion [pi] value</li>
</ul>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Their results are interesting because the=
y show how likely replication study are to fail, based on any p-value that =
is more than 0.001.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Quoting from <a href=3D"https://go.gale.c=
om/ps/i.do?p=3DAONE&amp;u=3Dgooglescholar&amp;id=3DGALE|A441882875&amp;v=3D=
2.1&amp;it=3Dr&amp;sid=3DgoogleScholar&amp;asid=3D2d74e422" class=3D"extern=
al-link" rel=3D"nofollow">their paper</a>:</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">The propos=
ed intervals can be calculated from the original P value alone, without oth=
er data. Importantly, they do not depend on the sample size of the study.<b=
r>
   For example, suppose a published study reports a two-sided test with P =
=3D 0.049. Using the calculator, the 95% confidence interval for [pi] is fo=
und to be [0.000085, 0.99] and barely excludes 1, which is the null value o=
f [pi] for a two-sided test.</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">This inter=
val and the variability it reflects are exactly the same whether the sample=
 size is 100 or 100,000. The prediction and power intervals require specifi=
cation of the relative size of the two studies, but not the absolute sample=
 sizes. </span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">Suppose a =
replication study of the published finding above was planned using the same=
 sample size. The 95% prediction interval for P, which will cover the actua=
l follow-up P value 95% of the time, is [0.0000021, 1], demonstrating how u=
ncertain the outcome of the same-sized replication study would be. </span><=
/p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">The 90% co=
nfidence interval for de facto power is [0.062, 0.951] and conveys a simila=
r message. For an initial finding of P =3D 0.049 with any sample size, ther=
e can be little confidence in the power of a same-sized replication study. =
</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">If, instea=
d, the initial finding is P =3D 0.001, the 95% [pi] interval is [0.00000015=
, 0.18]; the 95% prediction interval is [0.0000000013, 0.60]; and the 90% p=
ower interval is [0.377, 0.999]. </span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">Desp=
ite the fact that P =3D 0.001 is usually thought to be a highly significant=
 finding, nonsignificant results in a same-sized replication study cannot b=
e considered surprising. </span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">In fact, a=
n initial finding of P &lt; 0.00001 is needed to have 95% confidence that a=
 same-sized replication study will have 80% power; P &lt; 0.0003 is needed =
if the replication study is twice the size of the original study (Fig. 1 be=
low). Furthermore, trying to replicate an initial test result of P =3D 0.05=
 with the same sample size gives 50% confidence of having 50% power to dete=
ct an effect in the same direction, but a sample 79 times that size is need=
ed to ensure 95% confidence of having 80% power.</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><br></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img=
 class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external-resource" draggable=
=3D"false" alt=3D"Solutions for quantifying P-value uncertainty and ..." wi=
dth=3D"600" src=3D"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=3Dtbn:ANd9Gc=
RyvGCktg7LjdQMH2H2oO9-qwU6B7NbXbxBAA&amp;s" data-image-src=3D"https://encry=
pted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=3Dtbn:ANd9GcRyvGCktg7LjdQMH2H2oO9-qwU6B7NbXb=
xBAA&amp;s"></span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;text-align: left;"><span style=3D"color:#09=
326c;">Confidence limits for power based on observed p-value. Estimated de =
factor power (solid blue curve) and 80% confidence limits (dashed blue curv=
es)<br>
   for study 2 with sample size N2 based on an observed p-value from study =
1 with sample size N1.<br>
   Assumes a two-sided test at significance level =CE=B1 =3D 0.05. Dashed r=
ed vertical line is at p-value =3D 0.05, and solid gray horizontal lines at=
 80% and 90% power.</span></p>
<h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1.4Recommendedcomplementtop-v=
alue-theFalsePositiveRisk:"><strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span st=
yle=3D"color:#c25100;">1.4</span>&nbsp;</span></strong><span style=3D"color=
:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>Recommended</strong></spa=
n><strong style=3D"color:#09326c;">&nbsp;complement to p-value - the False =
Positive Risk:</strong></span></h2>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-a.Needfora2-stepprocess:"><st=
rong><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">a.</span> Need for a 2-step process:</s=
trong></h3>
<p>Based on the above, it is essential to use p-values with the utmost care=
. A good way to proceed with p-values is to:</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">1. Control=
 the messaging about p-value in all communications with the stake-holders s=
o as to avoid common confusions.</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">2. Complem=
ent the p-value with an estimate of the FPR (False Positive Risk, also call=
ed FDR for False Discovery Risk)</span><strong> </strong>which is the true =
probability that the result observed happened by chance, even when the p-va=
lue is so small and/or the sample size is so large as to be most likely tru=
ly significant due to a high power test statistics.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>Let's explore the reason for these two pieces of advice:</p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">1. Control the messaging about p-value in=
 all communications with the stake-holders so as to avoid common confusions=
.</span></p>
<p>The p-value is one of the most commonly misunderstood statistic, often r=
esulting in wrong conclusion being reached about the true significance of s=
ome result.&nbsp;</p>
<ul>
 <li>Never use p-values on their own. Any such claim would be a statement o=
f p(data/H0) which is of no interest and necessarily misleading.</li>
 <li>Actually never<strong> </strong>use the term significant in relation t=
o a p-value on its own.</li>
 <li>without giving first an estimate of the False Positive Risk (the true =
probability the the result observed by chance, see recommendation 2)</li>
</ul>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">2. Complement the p-value with an estimat=
e of the FPR (False Positive Risk, also called FDR for False Discovery Risk=
), even when the p-value is so small and/or the sample size is so large as =
to be necessarily truly significant.</span></p>
<p>While adding 'supposing H0 is true' to 'The p-value is the probability t=
hat the result observed occurred by chance' is at least technically correct=
, it still does not validate the statistical significance of the result. <b=
r>
  So effectively even a correct formulation of the p-value is in itself tot=
ally insufficient to deliver what is needed. One needs to go beyond the p-v=
alue for that.</p>
<p>A simple way to keep using the p-value while avoiding confusion and ensu=
ring that we assert properly the significance of the result is thus to:</p>
<ul>
 <li>State explicitly that '<span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">The calculation =
of the p-value is a first step towards establishing significance of the res=
ult, but is not sufficient in itself.'</span></li>
 <li>Then adds that <span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">'Significance of the p-v=
alue result is then checked by calculating the False Positive Risk, which i=
s the the posterior probability that the null hypothesis is true, or said o=
therwise that the result happened by chance'<br><strong><br></strong></span=
></li>
</ul>
<p>Effectively the p-value itself is only the first step in a 2-step statis=
tical process to determine the significance of a single experiment. The req=
uired second step is the determination of the FPR, which can be calculated =
online <strong><a href=3D"http://fpr-calc.ucl.ac.uk/" class=3D"external-lin=
k" rel=3D"nofollow">at this site</a></strong><strong>.</strong></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 75=
.2588%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 100.074%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fffae6 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fffae6">
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong><span style=3D"co=
lor:#0055cc;">Definition:</span><br></strong></span></p>
     <p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#c25=
100;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#352c63;">The False Positive Risk is the=
 the posterior probability that the null hypothesis is true:</span></strong=
></span></p>
     <p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#c25=
100;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#352c63;">FPR =3D p(H0/data)</span><br><=
br></strong></span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;"><strong>2-step process fo=
r the statistical significance of a single experiment using p-value: 'P-val=
ue + FPR':</strong></span></p>
     <ol>
      <li><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">calculate p-value</span></li>
      <li><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">suppose a given p(H0) =E2=89=A5 0.=
5 and calculate the corresponding False Positive Risk.</span></li>
     </ol>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">BOTH values need to be co=
nsistent with a significant result (at their respective level of comfort) f=
or the single experiment to be significant.</span><br><br>
       Note: For (2) 0.5 is a fairly neutral value but use some common sens=
e to decide if a larger value is warranted.</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"content-wrapper" title=3D"">
 <h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-b.FalsePositiveRiskandtestpo=
wer:"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">b.</span> False Positive Risk =
and test power:</strong></h3>
</div>
<p>Let's note that there are two main situations that will govern our relat=
ion to the test power:</p>
<ol>
 <li>In most Data Science applications at BNZ we design a model by using pa=
st events and the populations are therefore given. We have to use whatever =
clean data we can, while being careful of any sampling bias introduced by t=
he cleaning, and this results is a given test power.</li>
 <li>Still we usually run an experiment before putting such model in produc=
tion, so there at least we are able to aim for a certain power and populati=
on size. Additionally there is plenty of A/B testing in BNZ digital where s=
uch situation is the standard. In these cases where we are designing an exp=
eriment with data still to be collected, we can use our control on the popu=
lation sizes (within reasonable bounds). So one can estimate the sample siz=
e that will give a test power of close to 0.80 or even better 0.90 and use =
that size. See this A/B Testing <a href=3D"https://abtestguide.com/abtestsi=
ze/" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">calculator</a> and also <a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.evanmiller.org/ab-testing/sample-size.html#!0.5;80;5;10;1=
" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">this one</a> for the determinati=
on of the sample size.</li>
</ol>
<p>In situation (2) the standard in the literature is to suppose a '<strong=
>well-powered</strong>' test power of 0.80 for most examples.<br>
  In terms of Null Hypothesis Significance Test, this translates as a popul=
ation of 16 for an effect size of 1 stdev (using a Student t distribution f=
or small populations).</p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-c.CalculationofFPR:"><strong>=
<span style=3D"color:#c25100;">c.</span> Calculation of </strong><strong>FP=
R</strong><strong>:</strong></h3>
<p>In this section we will show that an exact calculation of the FPR requir=
es using a reasoned subjective value for the prior odds ratio.</p>
<p>But we will also show that in any case a minimal reasonable value for th=
e FPR can easily be derived.</p>
<p>This minimum boundary thus plays a key role in illustrating the best cas=
e scenario, as it has the advantage of not requiring any subjective value f=
or the prior odds (only a very reasonable assumption in a discovery test co=
ntext is required) while its value is itself already very telling as to the=
 limitations of p-values in many situations.&nbsp;<strong><br></strong></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><strong><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">c.1 Calculation of Likelihood rat=
io (a.k.a Bayes Factor):</span></strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Let's remember that formulation of Bayes'=
 theorem in terms of update via the <strong>Likelihood ratio</strong>:<br><=
/span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span styl=
e=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq2:&nbsp; p(H1/data)/p(H0/data)&nbsp; =3D p(data/H1)/=
p(data/H0) =C3=97 p(H1)/p(H0)</span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span styl=
e=3D"color:#0055cc;">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;posterior odds ratio=
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; =3D likelihood ratio&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;=
 &nbsp; &nbsp;=C3=97 prior odds ratio</span><br></span></p>
<p>The likelihood ratio is also be called '<strong>Bayes factor</strong>'.<=
br>
  Note that the Bayes Factor is totally independent of any selecting any <s=
trong>subjective</strong> value for p(H0) or p(H1).<br>
  The Bayes Factor is an <strong>objective</strong> transformation factor, =
that can be applied to subjective probabilities to see how these change whe=
n evidence is introduced.<br>
  The log of the Bayes Factor is also called the '<strong>weight of evidenc=
e</strong>' and is additive across independent evidences.<br><br></p>
<p>Additionally we can avoid selecting any subjective value for p(H0) for c=
onsidering that <strong>at most</strong> p(H1) =3D P(H0) in a discovery set=
 up.<br>
  From this we see that the Bayes Factor (H1 vs H0) becomes&nbsp; a maximal=
 value of the posterior odds (H1 vs H0).</p>
<p>Going back to Figure 1 above, one can show that more generally (see sect=
ion A.2 of <a href=3D"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.1=
71085" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Colquhoun</a> for details):=
</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq3:&nbsp;=
 &nbsp; likelihood ratio(H1 vs. H0) =3D y1 / (2 x y0)</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable">
  <colgroup>
   <col>
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e6fcff confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light teal 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e6fcff">
     <p title=3D""><u><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Illustration using Fig=
ure 1:</span></u></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">For a p-value of 0.05 wit=
h a test power of 0.8:</span></p>
     <ul title=3D"">
      <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">p(data/H1) =3D p1 =3D y1 ~ <strong=
>0.29</strong> and</span></li>
      <li><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">p(data/H0) =3D p0 =3D 2 x y0 ~ 2 x=
 0.0526 ~ <strong>0.105</strong></span></li>
     </ul>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">hence Likelihood ratio(H1=
 vs. H0) ~ 0.29 / (2*0.0526)&nbsp;</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">so Likelihood ratio =3D B=
ayes Factor ~ <strong>2.76</strong>&nbsp;</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Let's note that that valu=
e is much less than the false 20 derived from p =3D 0.05 (when one wrongly =
supposes that p =3D 0.05 is the probability that H0 is true).</span></p></t=
d>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>c.2 Boundary value: Derivation of=
 FPR when prior odds ratio is 1:</strong></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">One particular case of the posterior odds=
 ratio formula above when the prior odds are 1 (i.e. the prior probability =
of there being a real effect is 0.5).<br>
   In that case the he posterior odds are equal to the likelihood ratio (a.=
k.a Bayes Factor).</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Since it is not normally considered accep=
table to be optimistic about H1 to the point of supposing p(H1) &gt; 0.5 be=
fore doing the experiment, one can see that in all acceptable cases the pri=
or odds ratio ratio will be at least 1, so that likelihood ratio is effecti=
vely the <strong>maximum value</strong> of the posterior odds ratio under t=
he reasonable value domain for p(H1) (p(H1) =E2=89=A4 0.5)</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Hence</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span styl=
e=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq4:&nbsp; &nbsp; posterior odds ratio =E2=89=A4 likel=
ihood ratio</span><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">(H1 vs H0)&nbsp; &nbsp; &n=
bsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; for p(H1) =E2=89=A4 0.5</span><span=
 style=3D"color:#0055cc;">&nbsp;</span></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>From probability =3D odds/(1+odds), considering p(H0/data) and <strong>s=
upposing that the prior odds ratio (p(H1)/p(H0)) is1</strong>, we get</p>
<p>p(H0/data) =3D p(H1/data) / likelihood ratio&nbsp;</p>
<p>p(H0/data) =3D (1-p(H0/data)) / likelihood ratio</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq5:&nbsp;=
 &nbsp; FPR =3D p(H0/data) =3D 1/(1 + likelihood ratio)&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;=
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbs=
p; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; (supposing prior odds ratio =
=3D 1)</span></p>
<p>Which can also be written as:</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq6:&nbsp;=
 &nbsp; FPR =3D p(H0/data) =3D p(data/H0)/ [p(data/H0) + p(data/H1)] &nbsp;=
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(supposing prior odds ratio =3D 1=
)</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 87=
.8292%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 99.9971%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e6fcff confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light teal 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e6fcff">
     <p title=3D""><u><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Illustration using&nbs=
p;Figure 1:</span></u></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">For a p-value of 0.05 wit=
h a test power of 0.8:</span></p>
     <p title=3D"">FPR =3D p(H0/data) ~ 1 /(1+2.76), using eq5</p>
     <p title=3D"">or <span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">FPR =3D p(H0/data)~ 0.=
105 / (0.105 + 0.29), using eq6</span></p>
     <p title=3D"">Hence <strong>FPR =3D 0.266 (supposing prior odds ratio =
is 1)</strong></p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p><strong>c.3 General direct calculation of FPR and relation to boundary v=
alue</strong><strong>:</strong></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">One typically never supposes that the pri=
or odds ratio (p(H1)/p(H0)) is more than 1, i.e: that p(H1) &gt; 0.5, which=
 would be twisting the test in favor of the H1 result - a cardinal sin in t=
he context of testing for a discovery.<br>
   So the practical</span><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"> maximum for that =
ratio is 1.</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">More preci=
sely </span><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">analyses of replication result</=
span><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"> <a href=3D"https://www.nature.com/arti=
cles/s41562-017-0189-z" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow"> suggest <=
/a>that for psychology experiments, the prior odds of&nbsp;</span><em>H</em=
><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">&nbsp;</span><sub>1</sub><span style=3D"col=
or:#37471f;"> to&nbsp;</span><em>H</em><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">&nbsp=
;</span><sub>0</sub><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"> may be only about 1:10,=
 meaning P(H1) ~ 0.1. A similar number has been suggested in cancer clinica=
l trials, and the number is likely to be much lower in preclinical biomedic=
al research</span><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">.</span></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">Practicall=
y in Data Science when working on marginal relations with weak data, one sh=
ould be conservative and consider P(H1) ~ 0.1 as a distinct possibility. An=
d that's before considering other potential issues, such as p-hacking which=
 is rather common in these marginal situations.</span></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>If we do not ignore p(H1)/p(H0) the eq6 just simply becomes:</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq7:&nbsp;=
 &nbsp; p(H0/data) =3D p(H0) x p(data/H0)/ (p(H0) x p(data/H0) + p(H1) x p(=
data/H1))</span></p>
<p>or</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">eq8:&nbsp;=
 &nbsp; &nbsp;p(H0/data) =3D p(data/H0)/ (p(data/H0) + [p(H1)/P(H0)] x p(da=
ta/H1))</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">As the prior odds ratio p(H1)/p(H0) shoul=
d never be &gt; 1 in a discovery setup, and in most situation much lower th=
an 1, we can see that practically:</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span styl=
e=3D"color:#0055cc;">p(data/H0)/ (p(data/H0) + [p(H1)/p(H0)] x p(data/H1)) =
=E2=89=A5 p(data/H0)/ [p(data/H0) + p(data/H1)]</span></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">In other words the exact value of p(H0/da=
ta) over the reasonable range for the prior odds ratio is never less than t=
he value of p(H0/data) for prior odds ratio =3D 1</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">Hence the value of FPR given by eq6 acts =
as a minimum boundary for the exact value of the FPR given by eq7, over the=
 reasonable range for the prior odds ratio.</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 86=
.0127%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 99.9971%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#e6fcff confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light teal 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#e6fcff">
     <p title=3D""><strong><u><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Illustration u=
sing&nbsp;Figure 1:</span></u></strong></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">For a p-value of 0.05 wit=
h a test power of 0.8:</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">We start with an a-priori=
 estimate of P(H1) ~ 0.1 (prior odds ratio ~ 1/9) which is in line with a-p=
osteriori estimates for discovery tests in social sciences and rather conse=
rvative for drug treatments validation.&nbsp;</span></p>
     <p title=3D""><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">As per c1 above, p(data/H=
1) ~ 0.29 and p(data/H0) ~ 0.105, hence eq8 gives</span></p>
     <p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D"">FPR =3D p(H0/data) ~ 0.10=
5 / (0.105 + 0.1/0.9 x 0.29)</p>
     <p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;" title=3D""><strong>FPR ~ 0.765&nbsp;=
 &nbsp;(supposing prior odds ratio is 1/9)</strong></p>
     <p title=3D"">This is nearly 3 times the minimal bound of 0.266 given =
by a prior odds ratio of 1.</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-d.Practicalexamples:"><strong=
><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">d.</span> Practical examples:</strong></h3>
<p><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;">This </span><strong style=3D"lett=
er-spacing: 0.0px;"><a href=3D"http://fpr-calc.ucl.ac.uk/" class=3D"externa=
l-link" rel=3D"nofollow">link</a></strong><strong style=3D"letter-spacing: =
0.0px;"> </strong><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;">provides a calcula=
tor for the FPR. Please use it.</span></p>
<p><strong><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embed=
ded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"fals=
e" width=3D"468" src=3D"ae417f19b21936bd8c2439a37f60f6c7e3c57605bf0243b9ce4=
79c8ced8ac407" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image2022-=
5-17_14-49-8.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1652755750197&amp;api=
=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"25798=
5870" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attac=
hment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-17_14-49-8.png" da=
ta-base-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-=
content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" =
data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" heig=
ht=3D"237"></span></strong></p>
<p>For a p-value of 0.05 and a well-powered sample size (n=3D16, power=3D0.=
78), the FPR is:</p>
<ul>
 <li>76% if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.1 (adversarial)&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; (=
=E2=86=92 in this case we have an exact FPR)</li>
 <li>26<strong>%</strong> if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.5 (neutral)&nbsp; &nb=
sp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; (=E2=86=92 in this case we have the minimum=
 FPR over the reasonable range for P(H0), i.e. p(H0) =E2=89=A5 0.5)</li>
</ul>
<p>note: 26% in the best situation is much higher than the often wrongly in=
terpreted 5%. <span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">Basically there is no sound ba=
sis to reject H0 at that level of p-value</span>.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>For a p-value of 0.0079 and a well-powered sample size (n=3D16, power=3D=
0.78), the FPR is:</p>
<ul>
 <li>5% if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.5</li>
</ul>
<p>note: 0.5 is the most optimistic value for P(H1), so we typically need a=
 p-value that is less than <span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">0.0079 </span>to =
make sure that the probability of a false positive (seeing a size effect wh=
ere there is none) is 5%.<br><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;">This is a very =
small p-value</span><u>.</u></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>For a p-value of 0.005 and a well-powered sample size (n=3D16, power=3D0=
.78), the minimum FPR is:</p>
<ul>
 <li>24% if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.1 (adversarial)</li>
 <li>3.4<strong>%</strong> if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.5 (neutral)</li>
</ul>
<p>note: 3.4% is good but this grows to minimum 24% if P(H1) is 0.1 instead=
. Hence it is still a rather marginal level of confidence in the adversaria=
l case.</p>
<p>This was nevertheless the basis of recommendation <a href=3D"https://www=
.nature.com/articles/s41562-017-0189-z" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofo=
llow">published in Nature</a> to move the <strong>'significant'</strong> cr=
iteria to <strong>0.005</strong> while keeping the p-value, and to call any=
 result based on a p-value between 0.,005 and <strong>0.05</strong> as '<st=
rong>suggestive</strong>'.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p>For a p-value of 0.001 and a well-powered sample size (n=3D16, power=3D0=
.78), the minimum FPR is:</p>
<ul>
 <li>8% if one supposes p(H1) =3D 0.1</li>
</ul>
<p>Note: this is still high if p(H1) =3D 0.1 but start getting acceptable. =
One would actually need p=3D0.00045 to bring the minimum FPR to 5%.</p>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped relative-table confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 77=
.0209%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 99.9955%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#fffae6 confluenceTd" title=3D"Background color =
: Light yellow 35%" data-highlight-colour=3D"#fffae6">
     <p><strong><span style=3D"color:#c9372c;" title=3D"">Take-away</span><=
/strong></p>
     <p><span style=3D"color:#164555;" title=3D""><strong>#1.</strong> A p-=
value of around 0.001 and not 0.05 is compatible with a probability of 5% f=
or a false positive (seeing a size effect where there is none) within a rea=
sonable range for p(H1) and a common standard test power of 0.8.&nbsp;</spa=
n></p>
     <p><strong><span style=3D"color:#164555;" title=3D"">#2. </span></stro=
ng><span style=3D"color:#164555;" title=3D"">If p-value are to be used, som=
e authors have recommended that - as a lesser evil - at the very least the =
'significant' criteria should be moved down to 0.005, while still understan=
ding all the limitations of NHST.</span></p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p><strong><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper"><img class=3D"c=
onfluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" src=3D"ffb564b52006f1de915b93=
43ce0de563d466b3f4291fe5263372625c5cd85ceb" data-image-src=3D"/download/att=
achments/255050567/image2022-5-17_14-49-52.png?version=3D1&amp;modification=
