<p>(note: typically one does not consider p(H1) &gt; 0.5 out of necessary c=
onservatism, hence the red part of the curve above)</p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-e.ApproximationofminimalFPRva=
luethroughthelikelihood(BayesFactor):"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100=
;">e.</span> Approximation of minimal FPR value through the likelihood (Bay=
es Factor):</strong></h3>
<p>With the usual reasonable hypothesis that p(H1) =E2=89=A40.5, we can der=
ive an approximation for min(FPR) using the approximation for the Bayes Fac=
tor given in 1.5.b.</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;"><strong>FP=
R =E2=89=A5 1/[1 - 1/(e p ln(p))]&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; =
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; (p(H1) =E2=89=A40.5)</stro=
ng></span></p>
<p>The steepness of the <a href=3D"https://www.desmos.com/calculator/t6kjhy=
zqhf" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">FPR curve</a> (given for for=
 p(H1 =3D 0.5) is striking and perfectly illustrate why the p-value is an u=
nreliable guide.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p><strong><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: n=
one;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"m=
row" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-=
decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><=
span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"confluenc=
e-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"conf=
luence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" width=3D"468" src=3D"0220062867c=
019b946a7fe35d6de11f5bb485d571ae7912ae65d2871f87288ac" data-image-src=3D"/d=
ownload/attachments/255050567/image2022-5-23_11-6-51.png?version=3D1&amp;mo=
dificationDate=3D1653260811924&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolved-comment-count=
=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"258000592" data-linked-resource-version=
=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-defaul=
t-alias=3D"image2022-5-23_11-6-51.png" data-base-url=3D"https://confluence.=
nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-li=
nked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-linked-resource-container-ver=
sion=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" height=3D"373"></span></span></span></=
span></span></span></span></span></strong></p>
<p><em><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;=
color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow"=
 style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-deco=
ration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span=
 class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">Approximation of FPR minimal=
 value as a function of</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><st=
rong><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;co=
lor:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" s=
tyle=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decora=
tion: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span c=
lass=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"> p-value </span></span></span>=
</span></span></span></span></strong><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span s=
tyle=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoratio=
n: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=
=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D=
"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;=
">(calculated at P(H1) =3D 0.5)</span></span></span></span></span></span></=
span></em></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>This approximation is slightly above the values given in 1.4.d above, wh=
ich are based on the more precise <a href=3D"https://royalsocietypublishing=
.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.171085" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Davi=
d Colquhoun's approach</a>.<br>
  However the approximation is rather simple. It is developed also in <a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2685531" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"=
nofollow">Selke et al</a>, which was the basis of an oft reproduced illustr=
ation in a <a href=3D"https://www.nature.com/articles/506150a" class=3D"ext=
ernal-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Nature article</a> we saw earlier.</p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;"><span clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img=
 class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" height=3D"266" src=
=3D"2512f102991e244b646ff107ae5badbabd9e804005ffabc94f9aac5b0775cb0b" data-=
image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image-2025-1-29_11-43-46.png?v=
ersion=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1738104226802&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolv=
ed-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1548782705" data-linked-r=
esource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-=
resource-default-alias=3D"image-2025-1-29_11-43-46.png" data-base-url=3D"ht=
tps://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"i=
mage/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-linked-resou=
rce-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"468"></span>=
</span></span></p>
<p><br></p>
<h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-1.5Alternativecomplementstop-=
value"><strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;=
">1.5</span>&nbsp;</span></strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>Al=
ternative complements to p-value</strong></span></h2>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-a.ThereverseBayesianmethod:">=
<span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">a.</s=
pan> The reverse Bayesian method:</strong></span></h3>
<p>David Colquhoun proposed an <a href=3D"https://royalsocietypublishing.or=
g/doi/10.1098/rsos.171085" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">alterna=
tive approach</a> which does without having to consider a specific reasonab=
le p(H1) (=E2=89=A4 0.5): the <strong>reverse Bayesian method</strong>.</p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">Calculate =
what p(H1) prior probability would be needed to achieve a specified (desira=
ble) false positive risk (see the <a href=3D"http://fpr-calc.ucl.ac.uk/" cl=
ass=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">web calculator</a>). </span><br><spa=
n style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>If this prior is bigger than 0.5,</stron=
g> then you are <strong>not </strong>on safe ground if you claim to have di=
scovered a real effect. </span><br><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"><strong>I=
f the calculated prior is less than 0.5</strong> then it is up to you to ar=
gue that the calculated prior is plausible, and up to the reader to judge w=
hether or not they are convinced by your argument.</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">For exampl=
e, p=3D0.005 would require a p(H1) prior of 0.4 in order to achieve a 5% fa=
lse positive risk. So, if you observe p=3D0.005 and you are happy with a 5%=
 false positive risk, it is up to you, and the reader, to judge whether or =
not the p(H1) prior of 0.4 is reasonable or not. This judgement is largely =
subjective, and people will disagree about it. But inference has to involve=
 subjectivity somewhere.</span><br><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">Calculati=
on of the prior seems to me to be a better way than specifying an arbitrary=
 prior in order to calculate an FPR.</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<h3 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-b.TheBayesFactor:"><strong><s=
pan style=3D"color:#09326c;"><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">b.</span> The B=
ayes Factor:</span></strong></h3>
<p><strong>The <a href=3D"https://journals.lww.com/epidem/Fulltext/2001/050=
00/Of_P_Values_and_Bayes__A_Modest_Proposal.6.aspx" class=3D"external-link"=
 rel=3D"nofollow"> Bayes factor </a>is the odds of H1 to H0 given the obser=
ved data, basically the likelihood ratio of 1.4.c above. </strong></p>
<p>For example, a Bayes factor of 5 indicates that the strength of evidence=
 is five times greater for the alternative hypothesis than the null hypothe=
sis given the observed data.<br><br><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">A genera=
l controversy of the Bayesian approach is the need for you to specify your =
strength of belief in the effect being studied before the experiment takes =
place (the prior distribution of the alternative hypothesis).<br>
   This criticism of Bayesian statistics is often exaggerated because the i=
nfluence of the prior may be limited when a reasonable prior distribution i=
s used, and because there is also a default prior that one can use in the c=
ommon situation when one has little pre-study evidence for the expected eff=
ect size.<br></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;">Fortunately, there is a single, simple fo=
rmula that one can apply to convert a p-value to a form of the Bayes factor=
 without any other information. This simplified form, termed the <strong>Ba=
yes factor upper bound</strong>, states the most likely it is that the alte=
rnative hypothesis is true rather than the null hypothesis over any reasona=
ble prior distribution.</span></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" heigh=
t=3D"138" src=3D"924031d536c4e43b96deccd1f36f8dbe5b14ef471e30d99465f976b461=
671411" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image-2025-1-29_1=
1-52-6.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1738104726280&amp;api=3Dv2" d=
ata-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1548782747" d=
ata-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" =
data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image-2025-1-29_11-52-6.png" data-bas=
e-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-conten=
t-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-l=
inked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"4=
68"></span></p>
<p><em><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Lee and Wagenmakers=E2=80=99 classifi=
cation scheme for interpreting Bayes factors (BF</span><sub>10,</sub><span =
style=3D"color:#172b4d;">&nbsp;odds of H1 to H0)</span></em></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><br></span><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"=
><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-d=
ecoration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><sp=
an class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" =
style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoratio=
n: none;">For example, if the data produces a<span>&nbsp;</span><em>p</em>-=
value of 0.07 (sometimes termed a =E2=80=98trend=E2=80=99), the Bayes facto=
r upper bound is 1.98 and one can conclude that the alternative hypothesis =
is at most twice as likely as the null hypothesis (FPR =E2=89=A5 0.33).</sp=
an></span></span></span></span></span></span></p>
