<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;colo=
r:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" sty=
le=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decorati=
on: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span cla=
ss=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">Here is another calculation exam=
ple for a p-value of 0.05: </span></span></span></span></span></span></span=
></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D=
"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"te=
xt-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">B=
ayes Factor =E2=89=A4 ~ -1 / (2.718 x 0.05 x ln(0.05))</span></span></span>=
</span></span></span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D=
"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"te=
xt-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><=
strong>Bayes Factor =E2=89=A4 2.46</strong><br></span></span></span></span>=
</span></span></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;colo=
r:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" sty=
le=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decorati=
on: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span cla=
ss=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">And to show the link to the FPR:=
</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D=
"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"te=
xt-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">P=
(H1/data) / P(H0/data) =E2=89=A4 2.46</span></span></span></span></span></s=
pan></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D=
"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"te=
xt-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">(=
1-&nbsp; P(H0/data)) / P(H0/data) =E2=89=A4 2.46</span></span></span></span=
></span></span></span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D=
"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" style=3D"te=
xt-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: none;">P=
(H0/data) =E2=89=A5 1/(1+2.46)</span></span></span></span></span></span></s=
pan></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><strong><span style=3D"color:#37471f;"><s=
pan style=3D"text-decoration: none;color:#37471f;"><span style=3D"text-deco=
ration: none;"><span class=3D"mrow" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span =
class=3D"mstyle" style=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mfrac" sty=
le=3D"text-decoration: none;"><span class=3D"mo" style=3D"text-decoration: =
none;">FPR =E2=89=A5 0.289</span></span></span></span></span></span></span>=
</strong></p>
<p><br></p>
<h1 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-2P-hacking"><strong><span sty=
le=3D"color:#c25100;">2&nbsp; &nbsp;</span><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">P=
-hacking&nbsp;</span></strong></h1>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Always keep in mind the following necessa=
ry condition for the 2-step 'p-value + FPR' process to be valid, especially=
 when dealing with small samples.<br></span><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">=
This actually is largely true for any testing process:</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><strong>The experiment should be unique a=
nd not one cherry-picked out of a long series.</strong></p>
<p><br>
  This is one form of the so-called <a href=3D"https://www.nature.com/artic=
les/526182a" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Texas sharpshooter</a=
> problem, whereby one unknowingly develops a pseudo-scientific narrative a=
round a random result by ignoring the decisions that resulted in the select=
ion of that specific result.<br>
  This is extremely easily done by not recording these decisions and previo=
us attempts, including previous usage of the supposedly unseen data along t=
he way to this circling around a specific experiment.&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external-resour=
ce" draggable=3D"false" height=3D"150" src=3D"https://www.bayesianspectacle=
s.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/TexasSharpShooter.png" data-image-src=3D"h=
ttps://www.bayesianspectacles.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/TexasSharpShoo=
ter.png"></span></p>
<p>Also a simple serial attempt at significance tests for various populatio=
n statistics will eventually bring a false positive, if enough attempts are=
 performed.<br>
  This is not any different than saying that the max of n normal(0, 1) samp=
les is not 0, and the higher n then the deeper that max moves within the ri=
ght tail of the normal distribution. The max is a simple form of selection,=
 and any form of selection biases the measured value (the max here) away fr=
om the true mean (zero in this case).</p>
<p>The key idea is that the real experiment is the set of all the filtering=
, selection and eventual experiments attempted, including all the experimen=
ts that failed, and the one lucky experiment is just the necessarily biased=
 tail sampling of that distribution of individual experiment results, possi=
bly reinforced by unrecorded filtering and selections on the way.</p>
<p>A good example is social sciences where data dredging is rife (alternati=
ve hypotheses tried and proven false are not disclosed) and where unfortuna=
tely sample sizes are also small.<br>
  In such a set-up it is easy to get a marginal result by virtue or problem=
 #1 (when it is much worse than marginal) and then to push into supposed si=
gnificance by virtue of Problem #2 (we cherry picked it after many failed e=
xperiments).&nbsp;</p>
<p>Note that the same scenario can play in BNZ Analytics, when looking at v=
ery weak tests (due to small population sizes) and look scanning many featu=
res to try to find a separating one.<br>
  This is particularly dangerous when there is otherwise no strong partitio=
n - all the fishing we can do is the bottom fishing (data dredging) which i=
s bound to eventually return some artifact if we fish long enough and/or ca=
sually enough.<br><br></p>
<h1 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-3Structuralmodelissues"><stro=
ng><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">3&nbsp; </span></strong><strong><span sty=
le=3D"color:#09326c;">Structural model issues</span></strong></h1>
<p>Another <span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">necessary condition typically app=
licable to most significance statistical test is worth keeping in mind:</sp=
an></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><strong>The experiment is unbiased,</stro=
ng> so that the underlying comparison of some population statistic between =
different subgroups make sense.</p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external-resour=
ce" draggable=3D"false" alt=3D"home.cern,Diagrams and Charts" height=3D"250=
" src=3D"https://cds.cern.ch/images/CERN-HOMEWEB-PHO-2019-143-12/file?size=
=3Dmedium" data-image-src=3D"https://cds.cern.ch/images/CERN-HOMEWEB-PHO-20=
19-143-12/file?size=3Dmedium"></span><br>
  A good illustration of model bias was <a href=3D"https://cbms-mum.soe.ucs=
c.edu/lecture1.pdf" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">provided by Ja=
mes Berger</a> in the following slide where we can see how confidence inter=
vals shifted over time as model bias was slowly resolved, in such a way tha=
t the model bias shifts actually contradict the confidence intervals.</p>
<p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper confluence-embedded-manu=
al-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" heigh=
t=3D"363" src=3D"368cfea17e4ea04e43a79621c6fd3d9b1c2c9ef431d942d90dc7fc2d5b=
8b1a22" data-image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image2022-5-10_12=
-17-37.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1652141858726&amp;api=3Dv2" d=
ata-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"255053895" da=
ta-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" d=
ata-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"image2022-5-10_12-17-37.png" data-base=
-url=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content=
-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-li=
nked-resource-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"46=
8"></span></p>
<p>Structural modelling issues may create biases but may be very difficult =
to detect unless one uses at least <strong>two independent experiments</str=
ong>.<br>
  The 'two independent experiments' approach is actually a standard approac=
h in particular physics, with for instance two different detectors required=
 to validate key results at the CERN (typically ATLAS and CMS, <a href=3D"h=
ttps://home.cern/science/physics/12-steps-idea-discovery" class=3D"external=
-link" rel=3D"nofollow">two multipurpose detectors run by two different tea=
ms</a> so as to ensure true independence).</p>
<p>A version of the two-experiment approach is also the basis of <a href=3D=
"https://bmcmedresmethodol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12874-019-08=
65-y" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">some US <span style=3D"color=
:#172b4d;">Food and Drug Administration recommendation</span></a> regarding=
 the usage of p-values<span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">. <br><br>
   However typically BNZ Data Science applications do not lend themselves t=
o that <strong>two independent experiments</strong> approach, so we need to=
 keep in mind structural 'model' issues.</span></p>
<h1 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-4(non)Randomsampling"><strong=
><span style=3D"color:#c25100;">4&nbsp; </span></strong><strong><span style=
=3D"color:#09326c;">(non)Random sampling</span></strong></h1>
<p>Another <span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">necessary condition typically app=
licable to most significance statistical test is worth keeping in mind:</sp=
an></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><strong>Po=
pulations are randomly sampled, </strong>so that well defined test statisti=
cs may be used.</span></p>
<p>Most statistical tests (Student t for small populations,&nbsp; Chi squar=
e, etc) suppose random sampling. If that is not the case, the statistical c=
ut-off values used may be overly optimistic.<br><br><span style=3D"color:#1=
72b4d;">Of particular relevance here are <strong>possible structural data c=
leaning/filtering issues</strong> - basically is that data used representat=
ive (i.e. randomly sampled) after all the implicit and explicit filtering a=
nd cleaning done?</span><br><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Are instead bias=
es introduced instead? If so may these biases additionally differ across th=
e different populations considered for comparison?</span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">A good introduction to a more comprehensi=
ve overview of the problem was published <a href=3D"https://www.nature.com/=
articles/520612a" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">in a Nature arti=
cle</a> which is worth reading.<br><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-=
wrapper"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image" draggable=3D"false" src=
=3D"008f09697e478aa29d4b83f813e5558fb30b90454427e3e80efd5a58f0b47cf9" data-=
image-src=3D"/download/attachments/255050567/image-2025-1-29_11-48-26.png?v=
ersion=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1738104506961&amp;api=3Dv2" data-unresolv=
ed-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1548782727" data-linked-r=
esource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-=
resource-default-alias=3D"image-2025-1-29_11-48-26.png" data-base-url=3D"ht=
tps://confluence.nz.thenational.com" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"i=
mage/png" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"255050567" data-linked-resou=
rce-container-version=3D"108" tabindex=3D"0" alt=3D"" width=3D"306" height=
=3D"830"></span><br></span></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">Similarly some authors (see Greenland et =
al referenced below) also insist on the many assumptions that may lurk behi=
nd a p-value, including data dredging by ignoring intermediate failed direc=
tions and experiments:</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">We will ad=
opt a more general view of the P value as a statistical summary of the comp=
atibility between the observed data and what we would predict or expect to =
see if we knew the entire statistical model (all the assumptions used to co=
mpute the P value) were correct. (...)</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">The P valu=
e is then the probability that the chosen test statistic would have been at=
 least as large as its observed value if every model assumption were correc=
t, including the test hypothesis. (...)</span></p>
<p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;"><span style=3D"color:#0055cc;">Furthermor=
e, these assumptions include far more than what are traditionally presented=
 as modeling or probability assumptions=E2=80=94they include assumptions ab=
out the conduct of the analysis, for example that intermediate analysis res=
ults were not used to determine which analyses would be presented.</span></=
p>
<h2 id=3D"Pvaluesandothersignificancepitfalls-References:"><span style=3D"c=
olor:#c25100;"><strong>References:</strong></span></h2>
<p><span style=3D"color:#09326c;">A very good accessible book about the deb=
acle of the p-Value and, more generally, of Frequentist probabilities, is A=
ubrey Clayton's <a href=3D"https://aubreyclayton.com/bernoulli" class=3D"ex=
ternal-link" rel=3D"nofollow">Bernoulli's Fallacy</a>.<br>
   If the maths above do your head in, that book is nothing like that and a=
dds an excellent g<br>
   &nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><strong><span style=3D"color:#09326c;"> </span></strong></p>
